WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.962 position:63%
A red snapper snared in a net.

00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:05.888 position:63%
The shark homes in on the
distress signals, as the snapper

00:00:05.928 --> 00:00:09.191 position:63%
emits a faint electrical charge,
even when it's motionless.

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:18.876 position:63%
A millionth of a volt is
enough to stimulate sensory

00:00:18.976 --> 00:00:22.499 position:63%
organs called ampullae of
Lorenzini on the shark's snout.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.423 position:63%
In effect, the injured and the
weak sign their own death warrant.

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.444 position:63%
A grey reef shark
approaches the industrious

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:48.787 position:63%
White Tips, keen to
join Operation Cleanup.

00:00:57.605 --> 00:01:01.348 position:63%
Its impressive size does not earn it
the automatic right to muscle in.

00:01:02.189 --> 00:01:04.491 position:63%
This is a private party
for White Tips only.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.524 position:63%
Sharks themselves also run the
risk of getting caught in nets,

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:13.787 position:63%
particularly those more used
to inhabiting the open sea.

00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:18.271 position:63%
But white-tipped reef sharks are
equipped with two rows of teeth.

00:01:19.092 --> 00:01:24.957 position:63%
The uppers are triangulated and jagged,
the lower row narrow and sharp.

00:01:27.515 --> 00:01:30.999 position:63%
A perfect combination for cutting and
tearing their way out of trouble.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.862 position:63%
Damaged teeth will be replaced
in 18 days, and they'll

00:01:35.882 --> 00:01:39.263 position:63%
grow about 20,000 new teeth
in the course of their lives.

00:01:41.132 --> 00:01:44.453 position:63%
As long as white-tipped reef shark
move freely and hunt in packs,

00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.855 position:63%
neither nets nor other sharks
will stop them in their mission.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.536 position:63%
To clean up the lagoon of
injured and weak fish.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.371 position:63%
Inadvertently, the sharks will even
help dismantle these destructive nets.

00:02:03.693 --> 00:02:05.616 position:63%
This snapper might count itself lucky.

00:02:08.966 --> 00:02:10.828 position:63%
If only it could hide its distress.

00:02:11.649 --> 00:02:15.114 position:63%
The more it struggles to stay
afloat, the stronger the signal.

